# Flow Steps

A `step` is one named Action call in a Flow. A step has an Action target and a
data expression for its input. The Flow language accepts a small set of step
forms. It does not evaluate arbitrary Elixir expressions.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0"}])
```

## Define An Echo Action

This Action accepts any map and returns the same map. It is useful for showing
Flow syntax without hiding the data mapping.

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_steps_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end
```

## Explicit Names

Give a step an atom or string name. The input can be a positional expression or
the `with:` option.

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.ExplicitForms do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_steps_explicit_forms",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    positional_result = step(:positional, FlowSteps.Echo, %{value: input(:value)})

    named_result =
      step("named", FlowSteps.Echo,
        with: %{value: select(positional_result, :value)}
      )

    return(%{positional: positional_result, named: named_result})
  end
end

{:ok, %{positional: %{value: 3}, named: %{value: 3}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowSteps.ExplicitForms, %{value: 3}, %{})
```

The string name is stored as the canonical node name. The named step has a
result dependency on `"positional"` because its input selects that result.

## Binding-Derived Names

When a step is assigned to a binding and has no explicit name, the binding name
becomes the node name. The binding can then be used as a result expression.

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.BindingNames do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_steps_binding_names",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    first = step(FlowSteps.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})
    second = step(FlowSteps.Echo, with: %{value: select(first, :value)})

    return(second)
  end
end

%Jido.Flow{nodes: nodes} = FlowSteps.BindingNames.flow()
names = Enum.map(nodes, & &1.name)
true = Enum.all?(["first", "second"], &(&1 in names))

{:ok, %{value: 4}} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowSteps.BindingNames, %{value: 4}, %{})
```

The binding is authoring syntax. The canonical Flow stores a result reference
to the derived node name.

## Bindings, Shaped Inputs, And Returns

Bindings can be placed inside maps and lists. A return can also be a shaped map
or list. Every shaped value is built from literals and Flow expressions.

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.ShapedValues do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_steps_shaped_values",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    source = step(FlowSteps.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})

    shaped =
      step(FlowSteps.Echo,
        with: %{
          source: %{value: select(source, :value)},
          values: [select(source, :value), value(:constant)]
        }
      )

    return(%{
      nested: select(shaped, [:source, :value]),
      values: select(shaped, :values),
      literal: value(:done)
    })
  end
end

{:ok, %{nested: 5, values: [5, :constant], literal: :done}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowSteps.ShapedValues, %{value: 5}, %{})
```

A Flow must have exactly one `return` declaration, and the return expression
must include at least one step result. This keeps the Flow output connected to
the graph.

```elixir
{:error, missing_return} =
  Jido.Flow.parse(
    """
    flow do
      step :echo, FlowSteps.Echo, with: %{}
    end
    """,
    name: "missing_return"
  )

true = Exception.message(missing_return) =~ "return"
```

## Add Provenance Annotations

`label`, `tags`, and `note` are non-semantic annotations. They help tools
explain a Flow but do not change its execution or semantic identity.

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.Annotated do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_steps_annotated",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    annotated =
      step(FlowSteps.Echo,
        with: %{value: input(:value)},
        label: "Copy the value",
        tags: [:example, "mapping"],
        note: "Visible in provenance"
      )

    return(annotated)
  end
end

annotated_map = Jido.Flow.to_map(FlowSteps.Annotated.flow(), provenance: true)
[%{provenance: provenance}] = annotated_map.nodes

%{label: "Copy the value", note: "Visible in provenance"} =
  Map.take(provenance, [:label, :note])
["example", "mapping"] = provenance.tags
```

## Arbitrary Elixir Expressions Are Rejected

The authoring language accepts references, literals, maps, and lists. It does
not call functions such as `String.upcase/1` inside a step input. Parse source
with `Jido.Flow.parse/2` to see the validation error without compiling a bad
module.

```elixir
{:error, unsupported} =
  Jido.Flow.parse(
    """
    flow do
      step :bad, FlowSteps.Echo, with: %{value: String.upcase(input(:value))}
      return result(:bad)
    end
    """,
    name: "unsupported_expression"
  )

true = Exception.message(unsupported) =~ "unsupported flow DSL"
```

Use an Action for computation. A Flow maps data between Actions; it does not
turn a step input into executable application code.

